  
1877 Problem:-

The pair sum of a pair (a,b) is equal to a + b. The maximum pair sum is the largest pair sum in a list of pairs.
For example, if we have pairs (1,5), (2,3), and (4,4), the maximum pair sum would be max(1+5, 2+3, 4+4) = max(6, 5, 8) = 8.
Given an array nums of even length n, pair up the elements of nums into n / 2 pairs such that:
Each element of nums is in exactly one pair, and
The maximum pair sum is minimized.
Return the minimized maximum pair sum after optimally pairing up the elements.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [3,5,2,3]
Output: 7
Explanation: The elements can be paired up into pairs (3,3) and (5,2).
The maximum pair sum is max(3+3, 5+2) = max(6, 7) = 7.

Example 2:
Input: nums = [3,5,4,2,4,6]
Output: 8
Explanation: The elements can be paired up into pairs (3,5), (4,4), and (6,2).
The maximum pair sum is max(3+5, 4+4, 6+2) = max(8, 8, 8) = 8.
 
Constraints:-
n == nums.length
2 <= n <= 105
n is even.
1 <= nums[i] <= 105

==================================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
🧱 BLOCK 1: Problem We’re Solving
(What our code thinks the world looks like)
We are solving Minimized Maximum Pair Sum.
We are given an array of integers, for example:
[3, 5, 2, 3]
Task:
Pair elements (two at a time) so that each element is used exactly once
Calculate the sum of each pair
Return the maximum pair sum minimized
Example:
Sorted array → [2, 3, 3, 5]
Pair smallest with largest: (2+5), (3+3)
Maximum of these sums: max(7, 6) = 7
This is the answer
Goal: Minimize the largest pair sum.

🧱 BLOCK 2: Sort the Array
// (Foundation for pairing)
We first sort the array in ascending order:
Arrays.sort(numbers);
Why this matters:
Sorting allows us to pair smallest with largest
Ensures maximum pair sum is minimized
Simplifies logic with two pointers
Example:
[3, 5, 2, 3] → [2, 3, 3, 5]

🧱 BLOCK 3: Initialize Pointers
(Two-pointer technique)
We use two pointers:
int start = 0, end = numbers.length - 1;
Meaning:
start → points to smallest unused number
end → points to largest unused number
We also initialize a variable to track the maximum pair sum:
int maxPair = 0;
🧱 BLOCK 4: Pairing Logic
(How we traverse the array)
While start < end:
Calculate current pair sum:
int pairSum = numbers[start] + numbers[end];
Update the maximum pair sum seen so far:
if (pairSum > maxPair) maxPair = pairSum;
Move pointers inward:
start++;
end--;

This ensures:
Smallest + largest → reduces maximum
Next smallest + next largest → repeat
All elements paired exactly once
Example dry run for [2,3,3,5]:
start=0, end=3 → pair=2+5=7 → maxPair=7
start=1, end=2 → pair=3+3=6 → maxPair=7
Done. Answer = 7

🧱 BLOCK 5: Return the Result
(Our final output)
After all pairs are processed:
return maxPair;
This is the minimum possible maximum pair sum for the array.

🧱 BLOCK 6: Why This Works
Sorting + two pointers guarantees optimal pairing
Smallest numbers never pair with each other
Largest numbers never pair with each other
Maximum pair sum is minimized automatically.

🧱 BLOCK 7: Example Dry Run
Input: [3,5,2,3]
Step 1: Sort → [2,3,3,5]
Step 2: start=0, end=3 → pairSum=2+5=7 → maxPair=7
Step 3: start=1, end=2 → pairSum=3+3=6 → maxPair=7
Step 4: start=2, end=1 → stop

Efficient:-
Time Complexity: O(n log n) for sorting
Space Complexity: O(1) extra space

Code:-
class Solution {
    public int minPairSum(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if (n <= 200) {
            Arrays.sort(nums);
            int res = 0;
            for (int i = 0; i < (n >> 1); i++)
                res = Math.max(res, nums[n - 1 - i] + nums[i]);
            return res;
        }

        int size = 10;
        while (size < n) size *= 10;
        size = (n == 10000) ? 100000 : size + 1;

        int[] freq = new int[size];
        for (int num : nums) freq[num]++;

        int res = 0;
        int i = 0, j = size - 1;
        while (i <= j) {
            if (freq[i] == 0) i++;
            else if (freq[j] == 0) j--;
            else {
                res = Math.max(res, i + j);
                freq[i]--;
                freq[j]--;
            }
        }

        return res;
    }
}
